</ul>
</div>
</div>
<p><b>Let’s get into it.</b></p>
<div id="outline-container-emacs-ffap" class="outline-2">
<h2 id="emacs-ffap">FFAP &#8211; Find file at point</h2>
<div id="text-emacs-ffap" class="outline-text-2">
<p>For a refresher on Org includes for those who don’t know, I’d advise clicking on that link above or <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jSJev1fQ7Jc">watching my recent video on this topic</a>. Basically, includes let you include file contents from elsewhere in your system into a working document.</p>
<p>This can be useful for gathering individual files, like sections of a book, into a master document, or including bits of code from live files as examples.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="outline-container-jump-to-files" class="outline-2">
<h2 id="jump-to-files">Using ffap to jump to files</h2>
<div id="text-jump-to-files" class="outline-text-2">
<p>However, you may have noticed, these include lines don’t resolve as hyperlinks. So you cannot simply click an included file and jump to it.</p>
<p>This is where you can make use of the <code>ffap</code> (find file at point) command. It’s an interactive function that does exactly what it says: finds the file at your point.</p>
<figure id="attachment_1530" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-1530" style="width: 822px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class="wp-image-1530 size-full" src="https://chrismaiorana.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/find_file_at_point.png" alt="Emascs ffap demo" width="822" height="581" srcset="https://chrismaiorana.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/find_file_at_point.png 822w, https://chrismaiorana.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/find_file_at_point-300x212.png 300w, https://chrismaiorana.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/find_file_at_point-768x543.png 768w" sizes="(max-width: 822px) 100vw, 822px" /><figcaption id="caption-attachment-1530" class="wp-caption-text">The ~ffap~ command will present your file to you if the path resolves cleanly.</figcaption></figure>
<p>If the filepath under your point resolves cleanly to a file, absolutely or relatively, you will have the option to open that file presented to you in the minibuffer.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="outline-container-find-files" class="outline-2">
<h2 id="find-files">File paths passing and failing</h2>
<div id="text-find-files" class="outline-text-2">
